FAQCommon questions on this landscapeWho are the leading patent holders in automotive LED matrix headlight beam shaping?+Across the 50 companies in the ranked assignee list, the leader holds 7 of the 116 records in scope, and the top 5 combined account for 29.3% of all records. That means no single company dominates the field outright; the top 10 together reach 50.0% of records, so the remaining half is spread across a long tail of smaller filers. Practitioners should treat the leader board as directional rather than as evidence of a locked-up field, since a large share of activity sits outside the top ranks.Is the matrix LED headlight patent field growing or shrinking?+Filing activity peaked in 2018 at 13 records in a single year, and the most recent years that can be read as complete show a modest decline: 2021 had 13 records against 12 in 2024, an 8% drop over that span. Years after 2024 appear lower still in raw counts, but that is a publication-lag artefact — grants and applications typically publish around 18 months after filing, so 2025 and 2026 are not yet fully represented. Read the field as roughly flat off its 2018 peak rather than in active decline.Which patent classes cover matrix LED beam-shaping technology?+The dataset is dominated by two lighting-hardware classes: F21S (non-portable lighting devices) at 42.2% of the 116 records and B60Q (vehicle lighting and signalling) at 34.5%. Supporting classes include F21W (lighting use index, 16.4%), F21V (lighting device components, 12.9%), F21Y (light-source form index, 12.9%) and H05B (lighting circuits, 9.5%). Software and measurement classes are comparatively thin — G06F data processing sits at 8.6% and G01B measurement at 6.9% — which points to most claim activity sitting in the optical and electrical hardware rather than the control-software layer.Where should a new entrant look for open claim space in beam shaping?+The IPC composition suggests the control-logic and measurement side of matrix beam shaping — G06F data processing and G01B measurement, at 8.6% and 6.9% of records respectively — carries far fewer claims than the optical hardware classes like F21S and B60Q. That does not guarantee an easy filing, since a small class share can also mean fewer companies have bothered to claim there, but it is a reasonable first place to check for under-claimed territory before designing a new calibration or beam-control algorithm.Which jurisdictions see the most matrix headlight patent activity?+The United States receives the most filings in this dataset at 47, followed by China at 25 and the European Patent Office at 15. Germany, WIPO (PCT) and the United Kingdom each see single-digit counts (9, 7 and 5 respectively). This distribution suggests the US and China are the two markets where beam-shaping claims are most actively contested, and a freedom-to-operate check should prioritise those two offices before extending to the smaller venues.Answers are grounded in the same dataset. Derived from a Patsnap search on Automotive LED Matrix Headlight Beam Shaping Patent Landscape covering 2015–2026, data cut-off 2026-08-31. Counts reflect published records only and shift as new filings publish.Run this in Eureka MCP

